/-!
# The bounded local fibre-dimension locus is open and retrocompact

Source: Stacks Project, Morphisms of Schemes, Tags 02FZ and 02G0,
lemmas openness-bounded-dimension-fibres and
openness-bounded-dimension-fibres-finite-presentation
(blueprint: lem-trf-retrocompact-bounded-fibre-dimension).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- For a locally finitely presented morphism, the locus where the local dimension of
its scheme-theoretic fibre is at most `n` is open and retrocompact in the source.

Source: Stacks Project, Morphisms of Schemes, Tags 02FZ and 02G0
(blueprint: lem-trf-retrocompact-bounded-fibre-dimension).

Proof:
1. Local finite presentation implies local finite type by the affine pointwise criterion
   (lem-weil-pbc-lft-pointwise-criterion). Apply isOpen_localFibreDimension_le
   (lem-weil-pbc-local-fibre-dimension-open) to obtain openness.
2. Cover any compact open W in X by finitely many affine opens U mapping into affine
   opens V of S (lem-weil-pbc-affine-opens-basis). The restricted fibre is an open subspace
   of the original fibre (def-pc-scheme-fibre), so its local dimension agrees with that
   in X (lem-weil-pbc-dimension-at-point-open-subspace). It suffices to show compactness
   of the same locus for each affine morphism Spec B to Spec R.
3. The affine coordinate map is finitely presented
   (lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-opens-finite-presentation). Choose a stage of its nonempty
   finite-presentation approximation (lem-weil-pbc-limit-finite-presentation).
   Its fifth clause identifies B with B0 tensor R over R0, where R0 is finite type
   over the integers and B0 is finite type over R0. The affine fibre-product theorem
   (lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra) identifies the morphism as the base change
   of Spec B0 to Spec R0, with other projection q : Spec B to Spec B0.
4. Every ideal of the integers is principal: for a nonzero ideal, division by its least
   positive element gives remainder zero, and the zero ideal is generated by zero.
   Thus the integers are Noetherian (def-weil-pbc-noetherian-ring); finite-type
   preservation (lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian) makes R0 and B0 Noetherian.
5. Spec B0 is locally Noetherian (pqf-def-locnoeth) and compact
   (lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs). Its closed subsets satisfy the descending chain
   condition (lem-weil-pbc-noetherian-scheme-closed-subsets-minimal). Every subset is
   compact (lem-sfb-noetherian-space-compact-subsets), including its bounded local
   fibre-dimension locus. This locus is open by the local finite-type criterion and
   lem-weil-pbc-local-fibre-dimension-open.
6. Local fibre dimension is preserved by base change
   (lem-sc-fibre-dimension-after-base-change), so the desired affine locus is the
   inverse image under q of the compact open model locus. Both schemes are affine,
   hence q is quasi-compact (lem-weil-pbc-affine-scheme-qcqs and
   lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence). Its inverse image of that compact open is
   compact (lem-weil-pbc-quasi-compact-morphism-preimage-qc-open).
7. Intersect the original locus with W and use the finite affine cover in step 2.
   Each piece is compact by steps 3--6; their finite union is compact, including the
   empty cover. This is retrocompactness (def-weil-constructible-subset). Together
   with step 1 it gives the conjunction. -/
theorem isOpen_and_isRetrocompact_localFibreDimension_le {X S : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ S)
    [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f] (n : ℕ) :
    IsOpen {x : X | topologicalKrullDimAt (f.asFiber x) ≤ (n : WithBot ℕ∞)} ∧
      IsRetrocompact {x : X | topologicalKrullDimAt (f.asFiber x) ≤ (n : WithBot ℕ∞)} := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
